*(
    title: Natural Selection
    description: The mechanism Darwin and Wallace proposed, and the four conditions it requires to operate.
    tags: natural selection, Darwin, Wallace, speciation, module 3, biology, stub
    lesson
)

@include(src: ../preface.part.chalk)

scene{
    Natural selection is not a tendency of organisms to improve. It is an arithmetic consequence of four conditions, and wherever all four hold it follows whether anything is trying or not.

    Written out here: variation, heritability, differential survival and reproduction, and overproduction of offspring; the argument Darwin and Wallace independently arrived at; and speciation, where populations of one species stop interbreeding and become two.

    A mechanism this counterintuitive was not accepted on its plausibility. What convinced people is set out in *Evidence for Evolution*.

    internote(ref: evidence-for-evolution.chalk)
}
